_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gives the central conclusion: appointing a principal underwriter is a legal/operational role and does not by itself assure a sales force, marketing or asset gathering. It separates distribution functions (distribution arrangements, order processing, filings, best-efforts offering with no obligation to sell) from marketing, wholesaling and intermediary access, which may be separately contracted. It also says platform packages differ ('or included in a particular platform's package... Scope, fees, and actual effort vary'), matching the instruction not to assume packages are identical, and it recommends checking the specific agreements to see who supplies these functions, which aligns with the grader note's implication. It does not claim every sponsor must employ its own sales team. It omits the Simplify/Foreside example and the adviser-paid fee detail, but that is illustrative, and citations are not required. No material error.\"}","ttft_ms":3113,"type":"result","duration_ms":3131,"uuid":"43786741-b1e6-41c0-8802-f3b9042d0d79","ttft_stream_ms":602,"time_to_request_ms":46,"first_content_frame_ms":602,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
